WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.430 --> 00:00:04.650
Last video I talked about a strategy of betting
called the martingale system.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:09.640
It’s a strategy where you always chase the
total amount you’ve lost so far by betting

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.110
that loss plus 2.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:15.650
So that when you eventually win, you win 2
guaranteed.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:21.060
A lot of you saw the flaw in this system was
that the amount of money you’d need to sustain

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.040
it would be infinite.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.960
The issue is, even if you have a really huge
amount of money, as long as it’s finite,

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.760
the expectation value from playing this game
is 0.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:35.390
That’s because, with a very small chance,
you’d run out of money to keep betting and

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:36.390
you’d lose a lot.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:40.660
I didn’t believe this until I did the calculation,
because it seemed like you were so unlikely

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:45.399
to even lose many times in a row and so you
didn’t really need to worry about getting

00:00:45.399 --> 00:00:49.350
to the stage where you’d need to bet big,
so you didn’t have to worry about folding.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:53.700
But let’s do the calculation and see what
happens.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.040
Everyone has some number of losses, that would
mean they will have lost enough money that

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:01.620
they wouldn’t be able to pay the amount
they need to bet for the next round, so they’d

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:02.629
have to give up.

00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:06.950
Let’s call this number of losses for a particular
person, n.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:11.000
n can be very high or very low, it doesn’t
matter.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:17.150
The person only has a 1 in 2^n chance of losing
the maximum n times in a row.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:26.159
If they don’t lose then they win 2- that’s
most of the time, with a the probability (1-1/2^n).

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:32.620
But in the unlikely event they do lose, their
total loss is this sum of losses so far, which

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:37.190
we know is 2^(n+1)-2.

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:42.840
So we put all of this together in the expectation
value formula: the probability of not folding

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:49.659
times the gain plus the probability of folding
times the loss... and it magically works out

00:01:49.659 --> 00:01:51.450
to zero.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.909
The way to explain this is, when you play
this strategy, with very good probability

00:01:55.909 --> 00:02:01.740
you win make a modest gain, but with small
probability you will make a disastrous fall.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.190
It averages out to 0 in expectation.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:10.099
The higher your value of n, the less likely
you’ll lose to you when you play.

00:02:10.099 --> 00:02:15.550
as n approaches infinity, the chance you can
fail goes to 0.

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:21.400
In this version, where the person can always
afford to gamble more, they always make 2.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:27.109
As a lot of you pointed out, the casino also
has a maximum bet for this very reason- so

00:02:27.109 --> 00:02:30.470
everyone has to stop eventually.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:34.510
So this strategy clearly doesn’t work- if
you play it lots of times you’ll end up

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:36.699
with 0 on average.

00:02:36.699 --> 00:02:39.180
But what if you only played a few times?

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:43.349
And what if you capped your maximum bet at
a very reasonable price- so that you really

00:02:43.349 --> 00:02:47.189
wouldn’t mind too much if you did lose,
even though it does mean you’d lose more

00:02:47.189 --> 00:02:48.189
often.

00:02:48.189 --> 00:02:50.129
Let’s try an example.

00:02:50.129 --> 00:02:53.349
Say you set your maximum times of losing at
4.

00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:57.989
The probability of that is 1 in 16.

00:02:57.989 --> 00:03:01.749
So you’d think that you’d be pretty safe
if you only played a small number of rounds

00:03:01.749 --> 00:03:05.589
compared to 16, for example, 4.

00:03:05.589 --> 00:03:10.589
You’d lose money unless you won every round.

00:03:10.589 --> 00:03:17.010
But the probability of that is only around
77%- no were near a guarentee, especially

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.659
considering how much you stand to lose.

00:03:19.659 --> 00:03:21.829
Plus the amount you can win is just 8.

00:03:21.829 --> 00:03:26.719
In my opinion, you’d be way better off getting
a job.

00:03:26.719 --> 00:03:30.839
That solves the paradox entirely for the real
world as far as I’m concerned.

00:03:30.839 --> 00:03:33.699
But there’s still a paradox, in my opinion,
mathematically.

00:03:33.699 --> 00:03:38.389
Let’s look at the game where the person
paying never runs out of money.

00:03:38.389 --> 00:03:43.319
You might say, well that would require them
to have infinite money already.

00:03:43.319 --> 00:03:44.620
Not really.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:48.599
Each time they play, they only bet a finite
amount, and they can always borrow money to

00:03:48.599 --> 00:03:51.639
play because they’re guaranteed to make
it back.

00:03:51.639 --> 00:03:56.420
You might say, well, there isn’t even an
infinite amount of money in the world they

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:57.769
could borrow.

00:03:57.769 --> 00:04:02.349
Sure, but maths doesn’t care about what
sort of world we’re in -we could have been

00:04:02.349 --> 00:04:07.010
in a universe with different physics that
allowed the universe to be infinite, and the

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.709
maths would be the same.

00:04:09.709 --> 00:04:13.150
So this is the paradox, mathematically, for
me.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:17.250
This random red or black game should have
expectation zero.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:19.269
You just feel like that must be true.

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:24.250
There’s no way to on average make money
from a game of complete chance.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.669
But what’s the expectation of the martingale?

00:04:26.669 --> 00:04:28.960
Well it’s 2.

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.379
You always win 2.

00:04:31.379 --> 00:04:34.540
Or using the mathematical formula, we can
see the same thing.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:40.870
In fact, I can make this number anything I
want- for example, say I bet 50 more than

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:44.520
my total losses each time- then the expectation
is 50.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:50.069
But I can make it worse again, by using this
strategy, say you’ve lost a few times so

00:04:50.069 --> 00:04:57.569
far and it’s the nth round- then bet all
that you’ve lost plus 2^n.

00:04:57.569 --> 00:04:59.750
Look at the expectation value.

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:01.409
It’s infinite.

00:05:01.409 --> 00:05:05.909
So the real paradox for me is that you can
some how take a game that you think should

00:05:05.909 --> 00:05:11.790
have expectation 0, and then actually make
that expectation whatever you want, including

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:16.990
infinity, yet when you restrict to only ‘realistic’
cases, you get 0.

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:17.990
huh?

00:05:17.990 --> 00:05:23.010
This paradox showed me that my intuition about
expectation values is wrong- there are situations

00:05:23.010 --> 00:05:24.710
where it breaks down badly.

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:30.990
That’s why I really like paradoxes- if the
mathematics is sound, it means what’s unsound

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:33.560
is something in your own way of reasoning.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:34.869
It forces you to acknowledge that.

